Hi,

This patch adds a set of APIs to libsemanage for performing policy
management through the forthcoming policy server.  The majority of the
additions are in the new file "ps_api.c".

The policy server that will work with this API is still in development.
The current available prototype on Source Forge will NOT work with this
API, but we plan to release some updated prototypes in the coming months
for public inspection.

Note that this patch only provides APIs for manipulating policy
modules--installing, removing, listing them, etc.  The policy components
stored in databases are not addressed by this patch, except in as much
as some temporary initialization code is included to keep the associated
database functions from crashing if libsemanage is connected to a remote
server, and a warning is printed when invoking libsemanage in remote
mode.

Additional changes:

+ adds serialize/unserialize functions to module.c, to allow module
lists to be sent and received from the server.

+ renames private.h to byteswap.h, to reflect that file's actual
contents.  It also removes unused functions from this file that had been
holdovers from libsepol.


A future patch will be required to support the remote manipulation of
non-module components (users, booleans, file contexts, etc.) stored in
the "databases".  

The solution encouraged by the design of the database code is to create
the corollary of the *_file.c files in libsemanage/src/.  These would be
a set of *_ps.c files that would present their own set of flush/cache
operations to move the information over the wire instead of directly to
and from the disk.  Whereas the current policy server recognizes nine
mostly-module-related messages (CONNECT, DISCONNECT, BEGIN_TRANSACTION,
INSTALL_MODULE, UPGRADE_MODULE, BASE_MODULE, REMOVE_MODULE,
LIST_MODULES, and COMMIT), these additions would likely introduce 16
more messages, a flush and a cache message for each of the 8 components
(booleans, fcontexts, interfaces, nodes, ports, seusers, users_base,
users_extra).  This is modulo the removal of nodes and ports in favor of
iptables, which is an important issue in itself, because it introduces
challenges to the process of managing policy through one API.

Comments and suggestions are welcome on the strength and long-term
flexibility of this approach to the ps_api protocol.  Discussion is also
welcome on the challenges relating to iptables.

Christopher


